			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_569301473354084812">
				<blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Originally posted by <b><a href="#c569301473354076552">☢Paradoxic Gamer☢</a></b>:</div><blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Originally posted by <b><a href="#c568175523960729757">Scyrenus</a></b>:</div>Sure, but it still takes skill, effort, and intent to create good photos using a camera or to create digital art. If all you care about is more content, then it is fine. Heck, I even play idle games to get that serotonin running in my brain from time to time, but in general I care more about how things are made and what the artist is trying to convey. Otherwise, content for the sake of content is simply bland, degrading slop. There is a reason why some newer artists are returning to older methods of mediums in music, movies, and video games, because people still crave that old authenticity that gives art meaning. </blockquote>Just because something takes a lot of time or effort to produce doesn't mean it will appeal to people, like carving figurines out of wood requires considerable time and effort, but it's not like there are a lot of people who would actually want to buy these wooden figurines. Most people would rather buy a mass-produced figurine molded by a machine in a factory. AI is just another tool exactly like the camera, that enables people to create their own envisioned games. If the end product is good then it doesn't matter if AI was involved or not. </blockquote>Ayyy, thanks, now I want to visit some artsy craftsy stores to start making figurines.<br><br>Also, we're not talking about appeal here, people have always loved cheap slop even during pre-AI era.<br><br>And I do agree that effort doesn't always pay off, but it does teach you a lot even if it is a failed endeavor. If it is made by AI the final product is only a product, it inherently cannot be considered good or bad, it simply exists with no value attached to it. Because why would I pay for something if the developers didn't bother putting a part of themselves into the project?			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
